\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 22, 2007   5:37 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) is poised to approve a request to grow 3,200 ac
res of genetically engineered (GE) rice in Kansas. The rice contains human genes and would produce pharmaceutical substances. This action comes directly on the heels of the USDA?s preliminary approval of another company?s request to grow 1,000 acres of GE
 drug-producing safflower in Washington State. These requests represent huge increases in the acreage of drug-producing crops compared with earlier approvals.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--e
specially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamin
ation. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Roger Burton
\par 211 Lebrun Rd
\par Eggertsville, NY 14226-4121
\par 
\par 
