\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\fs20\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 Gerald Rubin
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 235 S. Walter Ave. , Trenton, NJ 08629-1810 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 19, 2007   3:19 AM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par I am writing to state my opposition to the proposed cultivation of pharma rice by Ventria Bioscience.  The economic benefit of growing pharma and industrial food crops outdoors pales wh
en compared to the risk and consequences of contaminating our food supply.  Cross-pollination is a well-documented phenomena and the possibility of spread of pharma genes to our edible crops is even more likely in tornado-prone Kansas.  Even if the chance
 of cross-pollination is not high, contamination is irreversible and it will spread - we simply cannot take the chance of having it occur.  I implore the USDA to ban all outdoor production of pharma and industrial crops.
\par 
\par Thank you for considering my views.
\par 
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Gerald Rubin
\par 235 S. Walter Ave.
\par Trenton, NJ 08629-1810
\par 
\par 
\par \page 
