\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 912 Rockwood Avenue , Bakersfield, CA 93308-1421 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 13, 2007   5:07 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination. Contamination is probably impossible to prevent once these crops are outdoors. 
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors -- especially in tornado-pron
e Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and is an ecnoimic threat to farmers who may be held liable for contamination. 

\par 
\par Therefore I strongly urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Robin Vosburg
\par 912 Rockwood Avenue
\par Bakersfield, CA 93308-1421
\par 
\par 
