\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 11, 2007   8:48 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par Industrial food crops and "pharma" are likely to contaminate the foo
d supply if grown outdoors especially in areas where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable 
for contamination. Unfortunately, experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par Drug-producing food crops grown outdoors pose risks to public health and and the economic well-being of farmers du
e to the likelihood of contamination to the food supply. I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops. Institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Barbara Hoffmann
\par 1701 Willow View Road
\par Urbana, IL 61802
\par 
\par 
