\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food sup
ply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be 
held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par Common sense tells me that to grow pharmaceutical crops out of doors is irresponsible and begging for disaster. Science is supposed to be responsible and to do no harm. This practice reeks of harm a
nd sorporate greed. Do NOT grow pharmaceutical plants outside. Certainly they can be grown in green houses where precautions of every kind can be instituted to ensure our FOOD remains free of contamination or is Frankenstein in charge.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Janne Bagley-Murray
\par 4749 Trumbull S.E.
\par Albuquerque, NM 87108-3550
\par 
