\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 10, 2007   12:56 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--es
pecially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamina
tion. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par Why not use the Kansas land for sound food crops badly needed by the nation.  There are so many small farmers who are so knowledg- able and willing to grow wholesome food for the country.  Industrialization is really not wa
nted by Kansas farmers and consumers.
\par Why is it being permitted for the sake of money.  Denial of a bad thing for everyone which Ventria Bioscience offers in Kansas, and a handing over of the land to small farmers to grow wholesome crops would be a wonderful move for the entire country.

\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Ken-Donna Bubb
\par 7450 S. Eastern Ave.
\par Apt. 2094
\par Las Vegas, NV 89123
\par 
\par 
