\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 2006 N. Emerson St. , Portland, OR 97217 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 10, 2007   3:05 AM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: No Drugs in Our Food Supply
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par I am very concerned about the fact that "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighborin
g fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. 
\par 
\par I'm especially concerned as I understand that this action comes directly on the heels of your preliminary approval of another company?s request to grow 1,000 acres of genetically engineered drug-producing safflower in Washington State. 
\par 
\par These requests together represent huge increases in the acreage of drug-producing crops compared with earlier approvals.  
\par 
\par Also, contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers' economic well-being
, as they may be held liable for contamination.  Significant increases, such as this proposal and other recent proposals, in drug-producing crop acres amplify the risks.  Plus, based on experience, neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trust
ed to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par Please, I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.  We have an obligation to our children and to ourselves to do no less.

\par 
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Amy Carlson
\par 2006 N. Emerson St.
\par Portland, OR 97217
\par 
\par 
