\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 9, 2007   9:30 AM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par Please look at this from an overall program point of view...not 
just rice in Kansas.  I would expect the USDA to create guidelines for "pharma" - which would preclude their growing anywhere outdoors.  I am a restauranteur in WV.  I am scared to death about the food supply.  If the food supply were kept pure and everyo
ne farmed with organic methods, NO PHARMA of any sort would be needed.  I think you guys need to back up and look at the long term...ask yourselves what is best for people.
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grow
n outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable
 for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Patti Miller
\par 1313 Apple Orchard Cr.
\par Berkeley Springs, WV 25411
\par 
\par 
