\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par All those who participate in such experimentation and profiteering, with zero regard for humanity, need to stop pretending they 'love' their children.  Better tell them they are not important compared to present day profits and the fun of doing r
esearch. When kissing their offsprind goodnight he children will NOT understand that there is a high likelyhood of their parents actually causing bio havoc and misery to future humans or even the child's own generation. 
\par 
\par This greedy meddling in genetics i
s risking the gradual deterioration of life itself in few years, since normal abnormalities which cause havoc have thousands of years to rebalance. The denial of risk is nothing less than criminal in the view of many geneticists who care about the preciou
s
ness of life as their first priority. The risks are numerous due to cross fertilization, remote pollination and many other hazards the executives prefer to be in denial about. This unnecessary tampering is a plain disregard for all people, species and a d
isgusting abuse of industry.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or w
heat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par George Thomas
\par 506 San Juan Ave,
\par Venice, CA 90291
\par 
