\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 Lois Granskog
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 \'d8ystein M\'f8ylas vei 45 , Trondheim,  N-7037 Norway
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 8, 2007   5:07 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am an American living abroad and I am concerned about the effects of contamination from genetically modified crops.  I am writing to urge the USDA to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow
 up to 3,200 acres of of genetically engineered drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if they are grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily carry seed
s far from the field they were planted in.  Animals can also be vectors for spreading seeds around.  Once released into the environment, this genetic material is very difficult to control.  This is clear from the recent  Clearfield CL131 contamination.   
C
ontamination from pharma crops may pose potentially serious risks to human health,  threaten farmers who may be held liable for contamination, and cause some of our conventional crops to be difficult to market abroad due to the risk of contamination.  Exp
erience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par 
\par Lois Marie Granskog
\par 
\par Trondheim, Norway
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Lois Granskog
\par \'d8ystein M\'f8ylas vei 45
\par Trondheim N-7037
\par 
\par 
\par 
