\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma ri
ce seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies 
can be trusted to prevent contamination.  Furthermore, prevention of contamination may not be possible.  
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on t
his practice altogether.  No genetically engineered crops should be permitted outdoos; the risk of contamination and creation of new invasive species is too great. 
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Augustin Parker
\par 36 Arthur Avenue
\par Marblehead, MA 01945
