\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par The FDA is about to approve the insertion of human genes into rice. 
 Are they nuts?  Yes it could make a lot of money for the companies lobbying for it -- Extracting the human genes and then selling them.  But the dangers of putting human genes into our food supply are categorically beyond the scope of profits.  The accid
ents would be like Exxon Valdez in our food supply, unknown allergic reactions, cancers, miscarriages.  There is no reason to use food crops.  
\par 
\par It is a dangerous gamble, the profits of the pharm industry versus the contamination of our food supply.  Econom
ic forces are not able to regulate the industry, as the crisis would be off the balance sheet.  This is the very reason for goverment, to pass laws safeguarding our citezens and homes from reckless intrusions.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely
 to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and t
hreatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.  
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Daniel Kling
\par 1142A Old york road
\par Ringoes, NJ 08551
\par 
\par 
