\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 8, 2007   3:09 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops will contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow phar
ma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat.  Serious risks to insect, animal, and human health as well as threats to farmers as well as corporations, who may be held liable for contamination, will result. Experience shows that neith
er the USDA nor biotechnology companies will accept accountablitlity, in the name of future job prospects,  short term profits and stock options.
\par 
\par The USDA should deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and institute a ban on this practice until such time that large, time consuming,  independant, double blind studies are conclusive.

\par 
\par 
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par mark walker
\par P.O.Box 220548
\par Brooklyn, NY 11222
\par 
\par 
