\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Deny the application for growing drugs in rice!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat.
\par 
\par Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that we can't rely on the USDA nor biotechnology companies to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether. It is not something that Americans should need to be subjected to.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Jenny Hayes
\par 7038 26th Ave NW
\par Seattle, WA 98117
\par 
\par 
