\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 David VanHouten
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 15508 West 127th Terrace , Olathe, KS 66062 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 8, 2007   1:54 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans,
 or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par This could
 conceivably harm many efforts at 'Organic Farming' as the practitioners would have no way to prevent contamination of their organic crops from pollution by genetically modified organisms. When you think about it, isn't it a pretty disgusting thought to e
n
vision eating something that is partially human or is a drug that you do not need? As far as the drug is concerned, if you are allergic to it, it could kill you. Until the genetic modification industry can prove, beyond a shadow of a doubt that there is n
o
 way that their 'crops' can 'escape' or cross-contaminate other crops, then allowing their growth under other than completely controlled conditions is totally irresponsible and potentially disastrous to all citizens of the United States. A world wide conc
e
nsus on controlling genetically modified food organisms would make the world even safer as pollen can be blown around the world, and crops from another country could ruin food crops globally. Just imagine a world wide 'potato famine' striking wheat or cor
n. Could we survive such a disaster? We can barely feed the teeming billions now and what with global warming and the looming disaster of it's by-products, can we afford to take anymore unnecessary chances with our food supply?
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par David VanHouten
\par 15508 West 127th Terrace
\par Olathe, KS 66062
\par 
\par 
