\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par There is no way to reliably contain genetically engineered crops planted in field plots. "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate t
he food supply if grown outdoors--especially in windy, tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens f
armers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Anne Millhollen
\par 2685 Benson Lane
\par Eugene, OR 97408
