\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 P.O. Box 153 , Whitethorn, CA 95589 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 8, 2007   1:07 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par I was shocked and dismayed by the news that bioengineered "Pharmaceutical rice" may be planted in Kansas. The argument that this is far away from other rice-growing regions doesn't hold water. Haven't we learned anything from th
e GE contamination of heirloom strains of corn in isolated Chiapas? It is simply not safe to assume that we can plant GE plants, and they won't contaminate the food supply. In all likelihood they will, and where then will we be? Already Anheuser Busch thr
e
atened a boycott of the whole state where the crop was originally planned. If the genes do escape, we will be watching other boycotts rolling in--from the EU, from Asia and South America. This is reckless endangerment of our economy and our health. Are we
 willing to put all of this at risk for the economic benefit of the creators of this transgenic rice? NO!
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could 
easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor bio
technology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Rosa Rashall
\par P.O. Box 153
\par Whitethorn, CA 95589
\par 
\par 
