\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par This is another important area where USDA needs to protect citizens from irreversible and reckless industrial pollution.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and in
dustrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serio
us risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I expect the USDA to act in the best interests of the gener
al citizenry first, and others after that.  USDA leadership should not have to be reminded of this, but sadly enough for our republic, this is clearly necessary.  The agency is not just ignoring the needs of the citizens, it is actively working against th
em, and this is intolerable.  These kinds of actions by authorities is the reason that this country was founded in the first place.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Julio Paz y Mino
\par 100 Lincoln Avenue
\par Havertown, PA 19083-3004
\par 
\par 
