\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par This is an outrage.  This is why we Americans are chronically ill with cancer and other types of illnesses.  Please grow food as close as possible to the way nature intended.  That is the proper way to get nu
trients from our food.  "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat
. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Dora Demers
\par 2202 Las Lomitas Drive
\par Hacienda Heights, CA 91745
\par 
\par 
\par \page 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\fs20\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 Joseph Jackson
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 345 Zoin St. , Hartford, CT 06106 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 8, 2007   12:33 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Toxic Drugs Out of Our Food!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par      "Pharma" and industrial food crops already ARE contaminating the food supply when grown outdoors!  In torn
ado-prone Kansas, the winds WILL blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat!  Experience has already shown that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can prevent such contamination, once the crop is planted.
\par      The 
previous placing of a pesticide producing gene in corn intended for nonfood use has already resulted in unintentional cross-pollenation, leading to the unintended death of billions of honey bees.  These bees are a "lynch-pin" species in our ecosystem!  Th
e
y are REQUIRED for the pollination of almost ALL of the food and vegetable crops of this entire NATION!  Such contamination poses potentially serious risks to our food supply, to human health, and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination
.
\par      Although it is extensively lied about and covered up, this OLDER problem is not yet even solved either!  We are still currently forced to IMPORT annually, billions of replacement honey bees from Russia and other foreign nations, that are beyond the r
each of this contamination!  The continuing presence of this "rogue gene" in other nonCORN food crops is still killing entire hives of bees all over this nation.  We do NOT need any NEW problems anything like THIS one!
\par      I urge the USDA to deny this and
 ALL  applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops, especially genetically modified ones, and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.  Limitation to indoor cultivation of these crops, in strictly controlled envir
onments, is the ONLY way to keep our food supply safe!
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Joseph Jackson
\par 345 Zoin St.
\par Hartford, CT 06106
\par 
\par 
