\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par This genie cannot be put back in the bottle once it escapes. I cannot say it better than the Union of Concerned Scientists, which I support, and who state the problem very well below. I can only add that this pr
oblem is not theoretical as it has already happened in other cases (see Monsanto v. Schmeiser). I am also from Nebraska and grew up in Iowa. Being from a farming state, you should be concerned about the effect on farmers and on the food supply.
\par 
\par "Pharma" a
nd industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially 
serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Joan Wallace
\par 1318 S. 51st St.
\par Omaha, NE 68106
\par 
\par 
