\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 Michael Roehm
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw60\brsp20 \wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 {\f36\fs22\insrsid7698630 1545 18th St., NW Apt. #209, Washington, DC 20036 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 8, 2007   12:17 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par So-called "pharma" and other industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors.  (1) This is especially true in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily blow
 pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. (2) Not only that, but birds will mostly likely spread seed and possibly be adversely affected.  (3) Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health, threatens farmers w
ho may be held liable for contamination, and could harm wildlife. It is unfortunate, but previous experience has shown that neither the USDA nor biotech corporations can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par Therefore, I reuqest that you deny this and all other such applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.  
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Michael Roehm
\par 1545 18th St., NW
\par Apt. #209
\par Washington, DC 20036
\par 
\par 
