\par }\pard \qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid7698630 March 26, 2007   5:27 PM}{\b\f36\fs32\insrsid7698630 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f36\insrsid7698630 
\par Secretary Mike Johanns
\par Secretary of Agriculture
\par U.S. Department of Agriculture
\par  
\par 
\par Subject: PLEASE Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par It is not speculation.  "Pharma" and industrial food crops are more likely than not to contaminate the food supply when grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where win
ds could easily blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USD
A nor biotechnology companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to act responsibly and deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops. Act now to institute a ban on this practi
ce altogether.  Initially, the idea of "pharma" crops was intriguing, but experience has shown that it is an idea whose time has passed.  
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Rebecca Backman
\par 19 Pomeroy Road
\par Andover, MA 01810
\par 
\par 
