\par 
\par Subject: Keep Drugs Out of Our Food Supply!
\par 
\par 
\par Dear Secretary Johanns,
\par 
\par I am writing to urge the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) to deny the application by Ventria Bioscience to grow up to 3,200 acres of genetically engineered, drug-producing rice in Kansas.
\par 
\par Our food supply is already comprom
ised by the use of poisons that replicate the effects of hormones, which have resulted in major changes in the development of our children, many of whom reach puberty at the age of 9 or 10; changes to their bodies with which they are nowhere near emotiona
lly ready to confront.  Now we are faced with the potential of further contamination of our crops.
\par 
\par "Pharma" and industrial food crops are likely to contaminate the food supply if grown outdoors--especially in tornado-prone Kansas, where winds could easily 
blow pharma rice seeds into neighboring fields of corn, soybeans, or wheat. Contamination poses potentially serious risks to human health and threatens farmers who may be held liable for contamination. Experience shows that neither the USDA nor biotechnol
ogy companies can be trusted to prevent contamination.
\par 
\par I urge the USDA to deny this and all other applications for outdoor production of pharmaceutical and industrial food crops and to institute a ban on this practice altogether.
\par 
\par Sincerely,
\par Mae Haynes
\par 15219 Holdridge Drive
\par Wayzata, MN 55391
\par 
